#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175761 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "e1c44d04-1798-5619-813d-a75e8f05140d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9087" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}5 - λ & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(5 - λ)(-2 - λ) - 4 \\cdot 2 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 3λ - 18 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 6)(λ + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=6\n$$\n\nSince we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175783 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "d60be4d7-335c-564a-bc75-52a2f76d660f" :question/math-academy-id "e-9225" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J$, we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving\n\n$$\n\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 - λ & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(2 - λ)(-4 - λ) - 3 \\cdot (-3) &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(λ + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175784 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "511398e8-780e-57b2-8f1b-092e9f6cca45" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Scalar Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "16a3033b-9110-5e1f-914d-8351bdc5f930" :question/math-academy-id "e-9226" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A$, and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176147 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b17622-2bed-5fdf-a001-6fed60a0e93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173545} {:answer/id #uuid "c4667d2e-b3e6-57a3-af29-52f2fb24c0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173546} {:answer/id #uuid "fd725588-9adf-5c5c-949f-ec236a9b9a91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173547} {:answer/id #uuid "3d63b09a-3934-5fe3-bcc2-33aac9c09e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173548} {:answer/id #uuid "3e409f37-33b3-5d46-ac67-07b2990b8f92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4667d2e-b3e6-57a3-af29-52f2fb24c0be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173546} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1148468-12b7-54d1-9cd0-e88e06b85167" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173544}] :question/id #uuid "7a50741c-c4f5-5a02-bb86-14c9da06d5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-108719" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 2x2 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a86e2a0f-c77e-5b27-9ed4-9c1a42bbf4c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173887} {:answer/id #uuid "e87ffe04-ed2a-5255-989a-938dc0eef949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answ